From be0ef70ed3df60330149e6d43db23eea16f8756f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zenium Port <port@zenium.invalid>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 07:36:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 92/93] video/mt6768: land ddp_mmp.c, the MMP layer the display code already calls into

display_recorder.c and ddp_drv.c have referenced five symbols with no provider since 0085:

  ddp_mmp_init  ddp_mmp_get_events  ddp_mmp_ovl_layer  ddp_mmp_rdma_layer  ddp_mmp_wdma_layer

They are the layer-mapping half of MediaTek's memory-protection profile hooks: each engine
asks ddp_mmp for the protection slot of the OVL/RDMA/WDMA layer it is about to program. The
declarations are in ddp_mmp.h, landed in 0085, and the calls in display_recorder.c are live
because the macro that guards them - SUPPORT_MMPROFILE, not a Kconfig symbol - is defined in
video/mt6768/videox/disp_drv_platform.h:37, which the port also carries verbatim. So this
round's slice is the file that makes those five references resolve: ddp_mmp.c, 934 lines,
verbatim out of 4.19.325 (sha256 prefix f0a113c93138), plus one obj-$(CONFIG_MTK_DISP_BRINGUP)
line in dispsys/Makefile, which goes from 15 gated objects to 16. Nothing else is touched.

The numbers below were measured by replaying this landing against the 91-patch tree and
linking the whole tree with the switch ON (portwork/logs/probe-ddp_mmp_c.log), then restoring
it, and the gate re-measured them on the landed tree:

  open names, switch ON     62 -> 57   (0089 78, 0090 65, 0091 62)
  names this closes          the 5 above, each open:0 in the link, defined:1 tree-wide
  names this opens           0
  ddp_mmp.o                 85,592 bytes, built from scratch
  diagnostics in ddp_mmp.c  0 errors, 0 warnings
  new global T symbols       6 (the 5 plus init_ddp_mmp_events), 0 collisions with the tree
  device tree               mt6768.dtb unchanged, appended DTB payload 493,517 bytes as since 0081

Zero new open names is the point, and it is not luck: everything ddp_mmp.c calls that this tree
does not have is inside a guard the port already satisfies one way or the other - the CONFIG_MTK_M4U
block at line 655, the CONFIG_MTK_HDMI_SUPPORT block at line 205 - and the mmprofile calls it does
make unconditionally resolve to the static-inline dummies in the landed mmp/mmprofile.h (:131
mmprofile_register_event, :212 mmprofile_start, :216 mmprofile_enable), which is the #else branch
of #ifdef CONFIG_MMPROFILE and matches this port's config of record, where that symbol is unset.

Why the MMP file and not RDMA/WDMA, which are the obvious next engines: they were priced the same
way, and both cost more than they pay. ddp_rdma_ex.c + ddp_wdma_ex.c + ddp_matrix_para.h compile
clean and close 10 names, but open 21 - 13 of them the record lifecycle, which in the vendor is a
3-4 line trampoline in cmdq/v3/cmdq_record.c into the cmdq_task_*/cmdq_op_* session engine (per-subsys
session pools, the gce_plat lock, mailbox submission) that this port refuses to land, and 8 of them
videox debug knobs that belong to the panel side of the cut. ddp_ovl.c closes 6 and opens 10;
ddp_color.c + ddp_dither.c + ddp_gamma.c would be net negative (8 closed, 1 open) but the one open
name is cmdqRecReadToDataRegister, and answering it means growing the record adapter with a fourth
entry point plus the register-typed-operand rule that 0091 declined - a decision, not a slice. All
four measurements are in report/l2-slice-0092-before-after.md with the vendor line numbers.

Two findings from that pricing are worth recording here because they stop a future round from
repeating the work. (1) common/rdma20/ddp_rdma.c and common/wdma20/ddp_wdma.c must NOT be landed:
video/common/Makefile:70-78 descends into those directories only for CONFIG_MACH_MT6799, and
DDP_REG_BASE_DISP_RDMA0, which ddp_rdma.c:25 returns, is defined nowhere in the vendor tree - they
are MT6799 code in a shared directory, like the cmdq/v3 .c files that this port carries as headers
only. (2) ddp_wdma_ex.c:19 includes <ion_sec_heap.h>, which this port has no way to satisfy without
dragging in the ION driver 0080 excluded; the include is redundant here (its only type use is the
ion_phys_addr_t declaration at line 1260, which drivers/staging/android/mtk_ion/ion.h:31 already
typedefs, and its only call is inside #ifdef CONFIG_MTK_TRUSTED_MEMORY_SUBSYSTEM) so when RDMA/WDMA
do land, the fix is the one-line comment-out already used at ddp_drv.c:36 - measured, not assumed.

The vendor also passes ccflags-y += -DDEFAULT_MMP_ENABLE when CONFIG_MMPROFILE=y
(dispsys/Makefile:109-111; even_defconfig:1712 sets it). This port does not, because its dispsys
Makefile carries no -D flags at all, and because with the define the guarded body of ddp_mmp_init()
is one DDPMSG plus two of those static-inline dummies - so it is recorded as a measured no-op rather
than silently applied. Adding the flag for one object would make it the only file in the directory
compiled differently from the other 16.

Honest limit, unchanged: nothing here draws a frame. The record layer this file's callers sit behind
is still link-required and runtime-unreachable - no landed code can create a record - and no
panel, backlight or boot has been touched. The state after this patch is "compiles and links with
five fewer unresolved names".
